course: dictionary
lesson: 13292
title: Granted: given, or agreed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Granted» is a word with a few meanings. The most common one is that something was given or allowed, often officially. «The teacher granted us extra time for the test.» The teacher said yes, so we were given more time.
explain: To take something for granted means to expect it to always be there and not feel thankful for it. «Don't take your family for granted.» The saying is a warning: your family is special, so say thank you.
explain: At the start of a sentence, «Granted» means «I agree that this is true», and a «but» usually follows. «Granted, the hill is steep, but the view is worth the climb.» The speaker admits the hill is steep, then makes their real point after the «but».
fact: Granted means given or allowed, often officially.
fact: Take something for granted means to expect it always and not feel thankful for it.
fact: «Granted» at the start of a sentence means «I agree that this is true», and it is often followed by «but».
fact: «Granted» can be a verb: the genie granted three wishes, so the wishes were given.
fact: Check the meaning by swapping in «given» or «allowed». If it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: «The teacher granted us extra time for the test.» What does granted mean here? | *Allowed, given officially | Thrown away | Painted in bright colours | Lost forever
exercise: «Granted, the hill is steep, but the view is worth the climb.» What does «Granted» mean at the start? | *I agree that this is true | It is a name for the hill | I am very angry | It is a question
exercise: Dad says, «Don't take your family for granted.» What does he mean? | *Do not expect them always and forget to be thankful | Make them wait outside | Sell the family car | Hide them away from the house
test: The genie granted her three wishes. What does granted mean? | *Gave or allowed | Counted | Broke | Forgot
test: Which phrase means to expect something always to be there and not feel thankful for it? | Granted that | Granted permission | *Take for granted | Wish granted
exam: «Granted, it was raining, but we still played outside.» What is the speaker doing? | Taking the rain for granted and forgetting it | Giving a wish to the rain | Asking for permission to play | *Admitting a point, then adding the real point after «but»
